meta {
  name: Revoke Invite
  type: http
  seq: 6
}

delete {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/users/{{invitedUserId}}/invite
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{ownerAccessToken}}
}

tests {
  test("returns 204 No Content", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(204);
  });
}

docs {
  # DELETE /users/:userId/invite

  Revoke a pending invite. The user row is **deleted** — it was never a person:
  never logged into, owns no records. Deactivating instead would leave a dead
  account in the directory and hold the unique `email` index against a future
  invite to the same address.

  **Module** — none (`@SkipModule`). **Permission** — `agency:users:write`.

  **Response `204`** — no body.

  **Errors**
  - `403` — caller lacks `agency:users:write`.
  - `404` — no such user in this agency.
  - `409` — the user has already accepted; there is no pending invite to revoke.
    Removing a real employee is **Deactivate User** (`DELETE /users/:userId`),
    which deactivates rather than deletes because an active user owns records.

  After this, the emailed link returns `404` from **Get Invite (Public)** and
  `401` from `POST /auth/accept-invite`.

  ## Why it runs last
  It is also this folder's **cleanup**. **Invite User** mints a fresh address
  every run; revoking it here means a repeated `npx @usebruno/cli run` leaves no
  accumulating pile of pending invites in the demo tenant. Same role as
  `Leads › Share Links › Create Disposable Share Link`.
}
